#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# appell_f1

#% text
# `appell_f1(a, b1, b2, c, x, y)` gives the Appell hypergeometric function `F1` of two variables.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `F1 = sum (a)_(m+n) (b1)_m (b2)_n/((c)_(m+n) m! n!) x^m y^n` for `|x| < 1`, `|y| < 1` (DLMF 16.13.1), continued analytically.

#% item
# Numerical values: the sum over the rows of one variable of Gauss functions `hypergeometric_2f1` in the other (DLMF 16.13 summed over one index), the variable of smaller modulus outside; the inner Gauss function continues beyond the unit disk. Values are given when `|x| < 1` or `|y| < 1`.

#% item
# At `x = 0` or `y = 0` the function is a Gauss function.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
appell_f1(2, 1, 1, 3, 0.7, 0.3)
#% output
#= 2.655223346206384

#% cell
appell_f1(2, -1, 2, 5, 0.4, 1.3)
#% output
#= 1.6306255323143872 - 5.025966113641617im

#% cell
n(appell_f1(1, 1, 3/5, 3, 2/3, 2/5), 30)
#% output
#= 1.5080453066835854866431438050103

#% cell
appell_f1(im, 1, 1 + im, 3.2, 0.5, 0.2 + 0.5*im)
#% output
#= 0.7953604048220261 + 0.03638414082093362im

#% cell
appell_f1(a, b1, b2, c, 0, y)
#% output
#= hypergeometric_2f1(a, b2, c, y)

#% text
# `F1(a; b1, b2; c; x, x) = 2F1(a, b1 + b2; c; x)`:

#% cell
appell_f1(0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.9, 0.6, 0.6) - hypergeometric_2f1(0.3, 1.2, 1.9, 0.6)
#% output
#= 0.0

